// =============================================================================
// world.fbs — voxel streaming and voxel edits.
//
// The server owns the world. It generates chunks, decides which ones a session
// can see, and pushes them; the client never requests a chunk by coordinate and
// never generates one locally.
//
// That same ownership governs changing the world. A client asks for an edit;
// the server decides whether it happens and then describes the result. Nothing
// in this file lets a client state that a block changed.
// =============================================================================

include "common.fbs";

namespace Voxelheim.Net;

/// One cubic chunk of voxels, run-length encoded.
///
/// **Voxel order** is x fastest, then z, then y:
///
///     index = (y * chunk_size + z) * chunk_size + x
///
/// This ordering is part of the wire contract, not an implementation detail:
/// the server encodes in it and the client's mesher indexes in it. Changing it
/// is a protocol version bump.
///
/// **Encoding**: `runs` is a flat vector of (block_id, run_length) pairs —
/// `runs[2i]` is the block id and `runs[2i+1]` is how many consecutive voxels
/// carry it, in the index order above. The pairs are flat rather than a vector
/// of tables because a table per run would cost an offset each, and a terrain
/// chunk holds thousands of runs.
///
/// Invariants a decoder must enforce on untrusted input:
///   - `runs` has an even length
///   - no run length is 0
///   - the run lengths sum to exactly `chunk_size^3`
///
/// A single run can span an entire chunk while `chunk_size^3` fits in a
/// `ushort` (32^3 = 32768 ≤ 65535). Raising `chunk_size` above 40 breaks that
/// and requires widening the run length — and therefore a version bump.
table ChunkData {
  coord: ChunkCoord;
  runs: [ushort];
}

/// The chunk has left the session's view distance. The client drops its copy
/// and despawns its mesh; it does not keep a cache the server no longer
/// considers visible.
table ChunkUnload {
  coord: ChunkCoord;
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Editing the world.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One voxel's address in **world** coordinates, measured in blocks.
///
/// World coordinates rather than chunk-plus-local, deliberately. An edit is a
/// point in the world; turning that point into a chunk and an index inside it is
/// the server's job, and the server already owns that conversion. Sending a
/// chunk coordinate plus a local offset would put a second copy of the same
/// arithmetic on the untrusted side, and two copies can disagree — leaving the
/// server to defend against the difference rather than simply perform the
/// conversion once.
///
/// A struct, not a table: three ints, always present wherever it appears, and on
/// the path of every edit. That is the argument that made `ChunkCoord` a struct,
/// and the same price applies — a struct can never gain or reorder a field.
/// Signed, because the world extends in both directions on all three axes, the
/// vertical one included.
///
/// The width is the same `int` as `ChunkCoord`'s, and that is what keeps the two
/// consistent: dividing any `int` block coordinate by a legal `chunk_size`
/// (1..=40, per `ServerWelcome`) yields a value that still fits an `int`, so
/// every voxel this type can address lives in a chunk `ChunkCoord` can address.
struct BlockCoord {
  x: int;
  y: int;
  z: int;
}

/// What a player is trying to do to a voxel.
///
/// Carries a zero member for the reason `ProtocolVersion.Unknown` exists:
/// FlatBuffers decodes an absent scalar as zero, and this enum rides on a
/// client→server message, so the zero value is the one that has to fail.
/// (`RejectReason` needs no such member — the server writes it, and every value
/// it can hold is a real answer.)
enum EditAction : ubyte {
  /// Never sent deliberately. A `BlockEditRequest` that omits `action` decodes
  /// as this, and the server refuses it rather than guessing which of the two a
  /// silent client meant.
  Unknown = 0,

  /// Reserved wire value for the direct break action withdrawn in protocol V2.
  /// A client must use `MineRequest`; sending this value in a
  /// `BlockEditRequest` is a protocol error. The numeric value remains reserved
  /// because enum values are append-only and old frames still decode to it.
  Break = 1,

  /// Put the item held in `BlockEditRequest.slot` at `pos`.
  Place = 2
}

/// A client asking for one voxel to change. **Intent, never outcome.**
///
/// The world's counterpart to `PlayerInput`: it says what the player is trying
/// to do, and the server decides what actually happens. Reach, line of sight,
/// whether the player is holding what they are placing, whether the chunk is
/// protected — all of it is resolved server-side, and none of it is stated
/// here. Breaking uses `MineRequest`, because the server must accumulate the
/// target's hardness on its own tick. A field the client fills in is a field the
/// server would only have to disbelieve.
///
/// **There is no rejection message, and that is the design rather than a gap in
/// it.** A request the server refuses produces no `BlockUpdate`, no error
/// payload, and no acknowledgement of any kind: the absence is the answer. So a
/// client must never treat its own request as applied. It may predict the edit
/// locally for feel, but the world it is entitled to believe in is the one the
/// server has described, and a request that goes unanswered means the world did
/// not change. There is deliberately no request id in this table, because there
/// is no reply to correlate one with.
///
/// Invariants the server enforces on this untrusted input:
///   - `pos` is a struct field, so an absent one decodes as null (Go) / `None`
///     (Rust). Reject that. The origin is a real location, so treating a missing
///     coordinate as (0, 0, 0) would silently edit the world at a point nobody
///     named
///   - `action` must be `Place`; both `Unknown` and the retired `Break` value are
///     protocol errors
///   - `slot` must be < `ServerWelcome.inventory_slots`. On `Place`, the server
///     reads that slot from its authoritative inventory and the item there must
///     name a block the server permits a player to place
///   - `pos` is unbounded on the wire and bounded by the server. An edit beyond
///     the player's reach, or inside a chunk this session was never sent, is
///     refused — silently, like every other refusal
table BlockEditRequest {
  /// The voxel the player is aiming at, in world coordinates.
  pos: BlockCoord;

  /// Place only. `Unknown` and the reserved `Break` value fail closed; see the
  /// invariants above.
  action: EditAction;

  /// The authoritative inventory slot to spend on a placement.
  slot: ubyte;

  /// The client's own tick counter, exactly as in `PlayerInput`: ordering and
  /// staleness only. The server never trusts it as a clock, and never lets it
  /// decide whether the edit was legal at the moment the client claims to have
  /// sent it.
  client_tick: uint;
}

/// One voxel is now this block. Server → client, and authoritative.
///
/// **One shape covers both directions of change.** A break is a placement of
/// `Air` (block id 0), so there is no separate break message: two payloads
/// differing only in which field is meaningful is how a union grows without
/// reason, and the union is the thing both sides have to keep exhaustive.
///
/// This is a statement about the world rather than a reply to anyone. The server
/// sends it to every session that can see `pos`, including the one that asked,
/// and it is equally the message for a change no client requested at all —
/// whatever the world later grows that moves blocks on its own is expected to
/// reuse this shape rather than add another. A client that reads a `BlockUpdate`
/// as "my edit succeeded" will be wrong the first time someone digs beside it;
/// the only reading is "this voxel is now that block".
///
/// A `BlockUpdate` supersedes the voxel it names in whatever `ChunkData` the
/// client already holds. Applying one to a chunk that has not arrived yet is not
/// an error to report back — there is no back — so a client either buffers it
/// until the chunk lands or drops it and trusts the `ChunkData` to arrive
/// already edited.
///
/// Invariants a decoder must enforce:
///   - `pos` absent decodes as null / `None`: a protocol error, not the origin
///   - `block_id` indexes the client's block palette, and an id this client does
///     not know is reachable whenever the server is the newer of the two. The
///     lookup is bounds-checked and falls back to a placeholder — never an
///     unchecked index into the palette, and never a dropped update
table BlockUpdate {
  /// The voxel that changed, in world coordinates.
  pos: BlockCoord;

  /// What stands there now. `Air` for a break.
  block_id: ushort;
}

/// A client asking for one chunk it has lost to be sent again. **A request for
/// data, never for an outcome.**
///
/// The counterpart to `BlockEditRequest` on the streaming half of this file, and
/// the weaker of the two: an edit asks the server to change the world, this asks
/// only for a copy of what the server has already decided the world is. Nothing
/// here states what the chunk contains, and the server answers with the chunk it
/// composes now rather than with the one the client remembers.
///
/// **Why the client can lose a chunk it is entitled to.** A client that cannot keep
/// up with the stream drops a chunk rather than holding one it knows is wrong — an
/// absent chunk is wrong nowhere, where a kept one accumulates every edit it had to
/// refuse. What that leaves is a hole in the ground, and the ordinary recovery is
/// slow in exactly the case that hurts: the server re-sends a chunk when the
/// coordinate is missing at the next view diff, view diffs are driven by **chunk
/// crossings**, and a player standing still crosses nothing. This message is what
/// ends that wait, and it is the whole of what it does.
///
/// **There is no reply, no acknowledgement and no rejection**, exactly as for
/// `BlockEditRequest`. An honoured request is answered by the `ChunkData` that
/// follows; a refused one is answered by silence. So there is no request id here
/// either — there is nothing to correlate one with — and a client must not treat a
/// request as a promise: it may ask once for a chunk it dropped, and then wait for
/// the world like anybody else.
///
/// Invariants the server enforces on this untrusted input:
///   - `coord` is a struct field, so an absent one decodes as null (Go) / `None`
///     (Rust). Reject that. Chunk (0, 0, 0) is a real chunk, so reading a missing
///     coordinate as the origin would resend terrain nobody named
///   - `coord` must be inside the requesting session's current view volume **and**
///     currently recorded as delivered to that session. A coordinate the session
///     does not hold is already on its way — the ordinary diff sends exactly the
///     chunks it is missing — so the request has nothing to add and is refused
///   - the request is **rate limited per session**, and the limit is the server's
///     own arithmetic rather than the client's. A resend is a cache read and an
///     encode in the cheap case; in the expensive one the server's chunk cache has
///     since evicted the chunk and it is regenerated, under a semaphore every
///     session shares. Trusting the client on what it asks for is not the same as
///     trusting it on how often
table ChunkResendRequest {
  /// The chunk the client has lost and wants again.
  coord: ChunkCoord;
}
